// PARAM: --analysis sign --context entry-state
// f is called twice from main with a positive and a negative literal
// argument. Sign's own entry-state routing keys the two activations of f
// by their entered p, so they stay two distinct contexts ([SPos] and
// [SNeg]) at f's single FunctionEntry node rather than joining into one
// shared summary. Each returned value propagates back per call site: x
// gets what the SPos-context activation actually computed for p + p
// (3 + 3, still SPos), y gets what the SNeg-context activation computed
// (-10 + -10, still SNeg) -- not join(SPos, SNeg) = STop at either check,
// which is what a context-insensitive analysis of the same program would
// be forced to answer for both.
void f(p) {
  t := p + p;
  return t
}
void main() {
  x := f(3);
  y := f(-10);
  __voblint_check(0 < x); // PROVED
  __voblint_check(y < 0) // PROVED
}
